# Code review checklist — SQL migrations

Before merging a Supabase migration, walk this list. Deeper guidance lives in the `project-supabase` skill and `supabase-security.mdc`.

## RLS

- Every new user-facing table **enables RLS** in this migration.
- Policies are present for the operations you actually need (`select`, `insert`, `update`, `delete`) — and **only** those.
- No `USING (true)` / `WITH CHECK (true)` on user data in production. If a broad policy is required (e.g. service-role-only access), document **why** in the migration.

```sql
-- GOOD: scoped to the owning user
create policy "trips_select_own" on trips
  for select to authenticated
  using (organizer_id = auth.uid());
```

## Indexes

- Columns referenced in RLS predicates and common query filters (`user_id`, `trip_id`, FK columns) have an index.
- Don't over-index: every index has a query that justifies it. Note the query in the migration comment when not obvious.

## Dangerous patterns

- No `DROP TABLE` / `DROP COLUMN` without an explicit data-preservation plan in the PR description.
- No `ALTER TABLE` that rewrites a large table without a migration strategy.
- No `GRANT ALL` to `anon` or `authenticated`; grants are minimal and intentional.
- No raw user input concatenated into dynamic SQL — use parameter binding everywhere.

## Reversibility / safety

- Destructive operations are gated behind a clear, named migration (so rollback is obvious).
- Foreign keys have an `on delete` policy that matches product intent (don't default to silent cascade for financial data).

If any item fails, **fix it now or file a Bead** — do not merge.
